[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Native SQL Queries and ResultTransformer / Tuple Mappings in Hibernate"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Hibernate ORM provides JPQL and Criteria API for database-independent querying, there are scenarios where writing raw, native SQL is necessary. Native queries are crucial for leveraging database-specific features, optimizing complex joins, or working with legacy database schemas. This tutorial explores how to execute native SQL queries in Hibernate and map the resulting datasets to domain objects, DTOs, or Tuples using ResultTransformers."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Executing Basic Native Queries"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To execute a native SQL query, Hibernate uses the NativeQuery interface, obtained via the Session.createNativeQuery() method. By default, if no mapping is provided, Hibernate returns a List of Object arrays."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"NativeQuery<Object[]> query = session.createNativeQuery("SELECT id, first_name, email FROM users WHERE status = 'ACTIVE'");
List<Object[]> results = query.getResultList();
for(Object[] row : results) {
    Long id = ((Number) row[0]).longValue();
    String firstName = (String) row[1];
    String email = (String) row[2];
}"},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"2. Mapping to Managed Entities"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"If your native query selects columns that match an entire entity, you can instruct Hibernate to map the result directly to your entity class using addEntity()."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"NativeQuery<User> query = session.createNativeQuery("SELECT * FROM users WHERE status = 'ACTIVE'", User.class);
List<User> activeUsers = query.getResultList();"},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"3. Using Tuple Mappings"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Instead of relying on positional Object arrays, Hibernate supports JPA's Tuple interface. This allows you to reference columns by their aliases, making the code much more readable and maintainable."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"NativeQuery<Tuple> query = session.createNativeQuery("SELECT id AS userId, first_name AS userFirstName FROM users", Tuple.class);
List<Tuple> tuples = query.getResultList();

for (Tuple tuple : tuples) {
    Long userId = tuple.get("userId", Long.class);
    String firstName = tuple.get("userFirstName", String.class);
}"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"4. Custom DTO Projections with ResultTransformer"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Often, you need to map query results directly into custom Data Transfer Objects (DTOs) rather than entities. Historically, ResultTransformers like Transformers.aliasToBean(UserDTO.class) were heavily used. Note that in modern Hibernate 6+, ResultTransformer mechanisms have evolved towards Tuple and explicit constructor expressions, but the concept remains essential."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"// Example using standard constructor expression inside the native SQL query
String sql = "SELECT new com.example.dto.UserDTO(u.id, u.first_name) FROM users u";
// Alternatively, utilizing ResultTransformer approaches in Hibernate 5/6
NativeQuery query = session.createNativeQuery("SELECT id AS id, first_name AS firstName FROM users");
query.unwrap(org.hibernate.query.QueryOperations.class)
     .setResultTransformer(org.hibernate.transform.Transformers.aliasToBean(UserDTO.class));
List<UserDTO> dtos = query.getResultList();"},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always prefer JPQL or Criteria API for standard CRUD operations to maintain database independence."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use column aliases (AS alias) when mapping to Tuples or DTOs to avoid index-out-of-bounds or mapping errors caused by database column reordering."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be cautious of SQL injection when concatenating dynamic parameters into native queries; always use positional (?) or named (:name) parameters."}]
